            <ArticleTitle>Virus subversion of protective immunity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>365-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The major histocompatibility (MHC) class I antigen presentation pathway plays a pivotal role in immunity to viruses. MHC class I molecules are expressed on the cell surface of all nucleated cells and present peptides derived from intracellular proteins to cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs), which then eliminate virally infected cells. However, many viruses have evolved proteins to inhibit the MHC class I pathway, thus enabling virally infected cells to escape CTL lysis. In this review, we summarize recent findings about viral inhibition of the MHC class I pathway.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
